Version 1.0 focuses on smoother play and lower memory use, especially on slower computers and mobile browsers. This release builds on 0.9.9 with further CPU optimizations, room-aware graphics loading, fewer loading hitches and audio interruptions, faster destruction effects, and shared resources for repeated items. Original artwork, individual lighting, gameplay logic and the port's deliberate improvements are preserved.

## CPU Performance

- **Reduced repeated work during play:** Hidden enemies and animated scenery keep their animation clocks and saved state current without repeatedly calculating bones and vertices that cannot be seen. Newly visible actors resolve their current pose before drawing; collision, projectile and attachment queries still obtain the exact pose when needed.
- **Made collision queries more selective:** Nearby-object grids and moving-world bounds select relevant candidates before the existing detailed collision checks. Moving doors, platforms, mounted props and animated scenery publish their current bounds immediately. Distant props no longer rebuild complete collision triangles every animation tick.
- **Reduced lighting and scene-update overhead:** Reused unchanged actor bounds, brush transforms and lighting matrices; narrowed Sun/dynamic-light candidate searches; and removed repeated resource-ownership scans. Original lighting selection, obstruction, collision masks and script order remain intact.
- **Reduced particle drawing and upload work:** Only the active portion of effect buffers is uploaded. Off-camera billboards, ribbons and flame trails skip drawing work while their particles, fading, random state and sound clocks continue normally. Large effects crossing the view remain visible.
- **Stopped redrawing a frozen level behind paused menus:** Menu/controller input remains responsive, and the world redraws when required by resizing, render-scale changes, pending loading or graphics-context restoration. Playing resumes immediately.
- **Measured additional improvements after 0.9.9:** A targeted three-scene browser comparison used approximately **17–26% less combined update/render-submission time** in the first follow-up, with a subsequent pass reducing its newer baseline by approximately **6–15%**. These are separate development-machine comparisons with 4× CPU throttling, not cumulative percentages or a promised reduction in a particular computer's CPU monitor. See the [browser CPU report](docs/browser-cpu-performance-followup.md) and [spatial-query follow-up](docs/cpu-spatial-query-performance.md).

## Room-Aware Graphics & Memory Use

- **Load the relevant rooms instead of every nearby floor:** Graphics eligibility now follows RedCat's current authored room, its immediate doorway neighbors, and farther views through aligned open doors. Unrelated rooms across walls, ceilings or floors no longer retain artwork merely because they are close to the camera.
- **Preserved complete large rooms and authored cameras:** Whole walls and moving assemblies remain intact, including the Tower's witch arena. Overview cameras and long camera transitions prepare their own view. Doorway preloading does not open doors, change locks or bypass puzzles.
- **Reduced distant preparation:** Nearby preloading now uses a **576-unit** radius, with an **1152-unit** retention radius and a bounded return-turn grace. Drawing uses a **2048-unit** nearest-bounds range. A large object whose nearest edge is visible stays whole, rather than being cut off at its center.
- **Load actor artwork on demand:** Distant actors keep their gameplay representation without immediately fetching every texture. Rejected rooms release unused GPU geometry and images, while shared player/effect resources remain protected. Unused decoded actor images expire after their idle grace.
- **Skipped distant renderer traversal and exact prop preparation:** Inactive graphics sit under hidden containers. Conservative animation envelopes avoid repeatedly sampling offscreen scenery; a relevant collision or rendering query still prepares its exact current geometry.
- **Restored original per-face visibility rejection:** Static scenery now submits only the faces accepted by the original BSP visibility data, using a reusable index buffer. Looking around does not rebuild world vertices or lighting arrays. Moving brushes and sky boundaries retain their complete, conservative handling.
- **Verified local memory reductions:** In the copied Castle basement view, room-aware selection reduced resident world vertices from **10,755 to 3,231**, chunks from **142 to 54**, and world textures from **32 to 10**, while preserving the visible room. A separate Forest check confirmed that the distant final section and Brutus artwork were not requested at the start, loaded near the arena, and released after returning. These are measured views, not whole-game memory guarantees. See [room-aware streaming](docs/room-aware-streaming.md).

## Smoother Room Loading & Audio Continuity

- **Spread graphics preparation across frames:** World chunks, actor/world textures and GPU buffers share a bounded preparation queue. Visible work receives priority without bypassing the budget. Shader variants prepare under the level-loading screen, and textures/buffers upload before their first displayed draw.
- **Prepare around camera turns:** The current room and already admitted, open-connected rooms complete background preparation in all directions. Recently admitted rooms remain prepared briefly while the same room and open connection remain valid, preventing small turns from repeatedly unloading and rebuilding the same section. Unrelated rooms stay excluded.
- **Prepare ahead of forward movement:** A bounded prediction of smooth camera travel starts loading the next sections before they cross the drawing limit. World scenery, actors and fragment artwork use the same preparation lead. Teleports and large camera jumps do not extrapolate, and drawing distance remains unchanged.
- **Preserved shared texture loads at doorways:** Newly wanted chunks acquire their image references before departed chunks release them. Room changes no longer cancel and restart an image decode still needed by the next room.
- **Removed audio dropouts from brief graphics waits:** A short readiness hold no longer stops and recreates every playing music/effect source. Sustained loading still pauses audio when the loading overlay appears, then resumes only that loading-owned pause. Explicit pause, Android suspension and asset-error behavior remain intact; sound selection, ambience ranges and music scheduling are unchanged.
- **Preserved complete views during unavoidable cold loads:** The last complete image remains on screen until required artwork is ready. Gameplay, recovery and save clocks stay held without a catch-up simulation step; missing scenery is not exposed to conceal loading.
- **Verified the reported local hitches:** Repeated left turns near the Forest's first Fleurifee lantern went from **12 held updates to zero**, with no audio source resets. Forward walking from the next copied save went from **six loading stops to zero**, with unchanged final residency. Representative doorway probes covered all five levels. These scoped checks do not claim that every device or uncached camera cut is hitch-free. See the [loading-continuity report](docs/room-loading-hitches.md).

## Destruction Effects & Particle Memory

- **Reused fragment rendering resources:** Crates, grave covers, glass, planks, the destructible gate, explosive rocks and knight armour now share a per-artwork pool. Nearby intact props reserve a bounded supply of prepared resources, and unused burst overflow expires after an idle period.
- **Prepared breakup shaders and textures before destruction:** Large crate stacks no longer repeatedly create the same materials, geometry wrappers and shader variants as fragments appear. Stable transparency passes also avoid changing material programs twice per draw.
- **Reduced fragment lighting and explosion bookkeeping:** Reused seeded explosion descriptions, unchanged posed bounds and collision/lighting candidates, while keeping each piece's independent trajectory, collision, spin, lifetime, transparency and lighting. Fragment counts, radial damage, destruction commands, chain reactions and saved debris state are preserved.
- **Improved the tested seven-crate destruction scene:** A live development-machine comparison increased average frame rate from approximately **200 to 325 FPS**, and the lowest rolling one-second rate from **84 to 215 FPS**. Native randomized fragmentation produced different piece counts in those runs; separate fixed-state checks verify equivalent physics and artwork. Individual frames and shorter windows can still fall below 200 FPS, so this is not a strict minimum-FPS promise.
- **Allocated particle/beam buffers to match actual use:** Small effects start with small draw buffers and grow to the existing maximum when needed. After ten seconds without visible submissions, unused GPU buffers are released and CPU drawing arrays shrink; returning effects rebuild immediately without restarting their simulation.
- **Measured lower effect-buffer storage across all five sampled levels:** Live vertex/index-buffer allocations fell by approximately **15–53%**, and effect drawing arrays fell from roughly **21–23 MB to 0.25–0.29 MB** in those scenes. These figures exclude textures, driver overhead and particle simulation data. Maximum-size effects retain their original capacity. See [effect and fragment residency](docs/gpu-effect-buffer-residency.md).

## Shared Models, Textures & Animation Data

- **Shared identical imports even when their filenames differ:** A generated resource index identifies matching geometry and exact PNG duplicates. Repeated items use shared immutable resources, while each actor retains its own settings, collision, rewards, animation state and script identity.
- **Kept animated poses and lighting independent:** Mutable vertices, transitions, opacity and native lighting uniforms remain per actor. Read-only clip preparation and compatible geometry data share storage without letting one room's resource cleanup invalidate another visible object.
- **Preserved original item differences:** The three money-bag variants share their identical body and duplicate images while retaining their separate **5/25/50-point** rewards and material settings. Collecting or hiding one item does not change another.
- **Measured savings with unchanged artwork:** The copied Castle view used **38 GPU texture objects instead of 54**. Counted actor geometry/animation backing-array storage fell by approximately **1.35 MB in the Graveyard**, with reductions in the other levels too. Paired views across all five levels were pixel-identical. This is resource sharing; draw-call counts are unchanged, and no new single-pass instancing renderer is claimed. See [shared actor resources](docs/actors.md#repeated-items-and-shared-resources-19-october-2026).

## Reliable Web Updates & Saved-Game Startup

- **Fixed a cached-code failure when continuing an existing save:** A browser could retain the 0.9.9 actor-residency module while loading newer world code, producing `this.actorResidency?.settle is not a function`. The generated import map now gives the complete JavaScript dependency graph a shared content-based revision, preventing that old/new module combination without converting saves or changing gameplay behavior.
- **Versioned all game modules and styles together:** The current graph covers **119 JavaScript modules**, including Three.js dependencies, and **three stylesheets**. Editing a nested dependency refreshes the URLs throughout the graph; unchanged source retains the same URLs for repeat visits.
- **Refreshed cache keys during source startup and packaging:** Source launch, the local server, desktop packaging helpers and Android asset preparation now regenerate the import map automatically. Added `npm run web:refresh` for manual website uploads and direct builder recipes. Updated the [hosting guide](docs/nginx-hosting.md#mixed-cached-code-can-look-like-a-broken-save) with complete-release upload and cache-revalidation instructions; matching entry HTML and source files still need to be deployed together.
- **Verified an upgrade with a real browser HTTP cache:** An isolated check first reproduced the exact error using the cached 0.9.9 module, then requested all 119 modules with the new revision. **Verder spelen** restored an older Graveyard save's exact position and health without browser errors or clearing user data. Loading left its saved bytes unchanged until the normal pause autosave; a narrow-viewport reload also passed. This checks cache separation and save startup, not a full campaign playthrough or a production deployment.

## Desktop Frame Rate & Version Information

- **Uncapped desktop rendering:** Electron can now render above the display refresh rate when hardware has headroom, while movement and gameplay remain time-based. The existing Wayland/X11 and graphics-backend choices are preserved.
- **Kept an optional synchronized launch:** Set `REDCAT_VSYNC=1` when starting the desktop game to restore display-synchronized rendering for lower resource use. Uncapped rendering can increase CPU/GPU usage; the monitor/compositor still determines what is physically displayed. Browser and Android frame pacing are unchanged. See [frame pacing](docs/native-refresh-performance.md#uncapped-desktop-fps--18-october-2026).
- **Updated version information:** The menu's **Over** screen now displays **1.0**, desktop package metadata uses **1.0.0**, and the Android wrapper uses version name **1.0** with an increased version code.

## Documentation & Verification

- **Documented the original loading system:** New executable research explains chapter loading, shared actor ownership, per-face visibility and the original Direct3D texture cache. The port retains its requested camera/room-based resource unloading because the inspected native paths do not provide an equivalent low-memory streaming policy.
- **Expanded reproducible performance reports:** Added focused CPU, collision, lighting, particle-buffer, fragment, room-selection, loading-continuity and shared-resource checks, with retained before/after source snapshots, measurements and images.
- **Preserved gameplay and existing saves:** These optimizations keep CPU level data, collision, scripts, offscreen enemy logic, buttons, pathways, animation clocks and save progress available independently of graphics residency. They do not introduce a reduced simulation rate, automatic graphics downgrade or new campaign mechanics.

### Technical Notes

- This document covers changes after the **0.9.9 release commit, `5f9a3f95`**. That release's native animation/combat work, explosion damage, audio-save continuity, initial CPU pass, campaign validation and standalone test rooms are not repeated as new features.
- Performance figures describe specific retained development-machine scenes. CPU timings, submitted frame rates, buffer allocations and texture-object counts measure different things; they are not interchangeable or guarantees for low-end hardware.
- Focused numerical, browser and Electron comparisons checked the changed systems, including actual copied-save approaches and all-level graphics probes. Those checks are not a fresh complete campaign playthrough, universal frame-perfect native parity, or validation of every platform package.
- Intentional gameplay fixes and sound/music scheduling remain in place. This release's resource pooling/sharing changes ownership and preparation, rather than reducing original fragment counts, changing actor lighting or merging independently scripted objects.
